---
import ChapterLayout from "../../../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../../../components/scenes/5.0/Hero";
import PrevNext from "../../../components/scenes/5.0/PrevNext.astro";
import { subPath } from "../../../data/course-nav";
---

<ChapterLayout
  title="5.0 — Chemical Age · AST 100"
  description="From roughly ten and a half to twelve and a half billion years after the Big Bang — the era when atoms learn to combine. Seven milestones from the first prebiotic molecules to the oxygen crisis, and four lessons: the periodic table as a map of cosmic history, the making of oceans and air, the origin of life, and the search for it elsewhere."
  activeId="5.0"
  chapter={5}
  current="/chapter/5/5.0"
>
  <Hero
    tease="The Planetary Age built the worlds; the Chemical Age fills them with molecules — and, on at least one of them, with life. Between roughly ten and a half and twelve and a half billion years after the Big Bang, atoms forged in stars combine into water, air, and organic chemistry; chemistry becomes a cell; and that cell poisons the whole planet with oxygen. Four lessons, seven milestones, and the sharpest question in science: has it happened anywhere else?"
    client:load
  />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="atoms">The Atoms and Their Addresses</h2>
      <p>The <strong>Chemical Age</strong> — Chapter 5 of <em>Our Cosmic History</em> — is where the story stops being
        about objects and starts being about <em>substances</em>. It picks up from the Planetary Age
        (<a href={subPath("4.3")}>§4.3</a>) with a young Earth of raw rock and metal, and it ends, about two billion
        years later, with a living, breathing planet. Across <strong>four lessons</strong> and <strong>seven
        milestones</strong>, it follows atoms into molecules, molecules into cells, and cells into an atmosphere.</p>
      <p>It begins with an inventory. The <strong>periodic table</strong> organises every element by a single number —
        the protons in its nucleus — but colour it by where each element was <em>manufactured</em> and it becomes a map
        of everything the course has covered so far: hydrogen from the Big Bang, carbon from dying stars, oxygen from
        exploding ones, gold from colliding neutron stars. The atoms in your hand are a receipt for fourteen billion
        years.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("5.1")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">5.1</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">The Periodic Table</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="molecules">Molecules, Air, and Sea</h2>
      <p>Long before Earth existed, those atoms were already combining. In the cold clouds between the stars — the
        clouds that ALMA, this chapter's telescope, was built to see — astronomers detect water, alcohols, sugars, and
        the carbon-nitrogen molecules that are precursors to the building blocks of life. The
        <strong>synthesis of prebiotic molecules</strong> (10.5–11 Gyr) is not an Earth story at all: it is a galactic
        one, and asteroids and meteorites delivered a sample of it to the young planet ready-made.</p>
      <p>On Earth, the raw materials became a world. Volcanoes exhaled steam, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen; the steam
        rained out for centuries and filled the basins with the first <strong>oceans</strong>. There was no oxygen in
        that air whatsoever — the air we call normal is a later invention, and life is what invented it.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("5.2")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">5.2</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Formation of Oceans and Atmospheres</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="life">Chemistry Becomes Life</h2>
      <p>Then the central mystery of the chapter — arguably of the course. In hot alkaline vents on the Hadean sea
        floor, a natural battery of protons drives carbon dioxide and hydrogen together over mineral catalysts. Fatty
        acids close into hollow bubbles: the <strong>formation of protocells</strong> (11 Gyr). Inside them, molecules
        appear that can both carry information and speed up reactions — the <strong>"RNA world"</strong> (11 Gyr) — and
        chemistry crosses, by a route we still cannot fully retrace, into biology. By around <strong>11.5 Gyr</strong>
        the first <strong>prokaryotes</strong> — simple cells without a nucleus — are established, descendants of a
        single ancestral cell we call LUCA.</p>
      <p>What follows is the biggest thing microbes have ever done. With the <strong>invention of
        photosynthesis</strong> (12 Gyr), cyanobacteria learn to eat sunlight and split water, throwing away the
        leftover oxygen. For hundreds of millions of years the ocean's dissolved iron swallows every molecule of it —
        rusting, sinking, laying down the banded iron we mine today. Then the sponge fills, and in the <strong>oxygen
        crisis</strong> (12.2 Gyr) the gas floods the air: poison to nearly everything then alive, and the fuel of
        everything that came after. By <strong>12.5 Gyr</strong>, <strong>eukaryotic symbiosis</strong> — one cell
        swallowing another and keeping it — builds the complex cell that Chapter 6 is made of.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("5.3")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">5.3</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Origin of Life on Earth</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="elsewhere">And Anywhere Else?</h2>
      <p>Life appeared on Earth almost as soon as the planet could hold it — which either means life is easy, and the
        Galaxy is full of it, or means we are the beneficiaries of one staggering accident. The chapter closes by taking
        the question outward: the <strong>habitable zone</strong> where liquid water survives, the few dozen rocky
        candidate worlds we have found inside one, and the way starlight filtered through a distant atmosphere could
        betray the presence of life — oxygen and methane together, two gases that destroy each other, so that finding
        both means something is busily making both. As of 2026 we have the telescopes, the method, the targets — and no
        confirmed detection at all.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("5.4")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">5.4</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Life on Other Planets</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <hr />

      <blockquote><p>The Planetary Age made the stage; the Chemical Age wrote the chemistry — atoms into molecules,
        molecules into a cell, and a cell that rebuilt the sky. Everything alive descends from that one experiment, and
        we still do not know whether it has ever been repeated.</p></blockquote>
    </div>
  </article>

  <PrevNext />
</ChapterLayout>
